---
title: Search
summary: One search engine, two ways to run it. Ship a static index to an on-device worker, or query it on the server. A no-JS fallback page comes for free.
related:
  - content/remotes
  - content/emit
  - content/shell
---

The site layer builds a search index from the **section documents** of your collections: the prose split by heading. Thus a hit lands on the exact section, not only the page. You pick where the query runs: on the server, or on the device of the visitor, against a static index. **The search on this site is the second kind**: a worker over a prerendered file. Thus a query costs no server round-trip.

::: tip When to use
Each docs site gets value from search. Prefer the static-index path for a prerendered site: it is free to serve, and operates offline. Use the server query when the corpus is private, very large, or changes per request.
:::

## Two paths, one engine

::: code-group

```ts [static index (recommended)]
// src/routes/docs/search.json/+server.ts — the prerendered index
import { docs } from '$lib/site.server';
export const prerender = true;
export const GET = docs.emit.search();
```

```ts [server query]
// src/lib/site.remote.ts — when the index shouldn't ship
import { remotes } from 'ogygia/content/server';
import { docs } from './site.server';
export const { search } = remotes(docs, { base: '/docs' });
```

:::

The same engine (`docs.search`), the same section documents, the same ranking. Only the location differs.

## The palette

`<Search>` is the ⌘K palette. It is an [island](/docs/regions/client-islands) that loads on demand:

```svelte
<script>
	import { Search } from 'ogygia/content';
</script>

<Search base="/docs" placeholder="Search the docs" />
```

The server renders only the trigger: a real link to the fallback page. On the first *intent* (⌘K, `/`, or a click), it loads the palette, and starts an [Orama](https://orama.com) worker that fetches and indexes the static file **off the main thread**. Ranking marks matched terms, and boosts title matches. No server touches a query. Nothing heavy loads for a visitor who never searches.

Orama is an **optional peer**: installed by the scaffold, and loaded only on the search path:

::: code-group

```bash [npm]
npm i @orama/orama
```

```bash [pnpm]
pnpm add @orama/orama
```

:::

## The server path, rendered

When you query over the wire in its place, the page is a few lines. The same hits, the same shape:

```svelte
<script>
	import { search } from '$lib/site.remote';
	let q = $state('');
</script>

<input bind:value={q} />
{#each await search(q) as hit}
	<a href={hit.href}>{hit.title} — {hit.excerpt}</a>
{/each}
```

## The no-JS fallback

Search must operate without JavaScript. The `<Search>` trigger is a real `<a href="/docs/search">`. With JS, it opens the palette. Without JS, it follows the link to a plain results page that you render from a server load:

```ts title=src/routes/docs/search/+page.server.ts
import { docs } from '$lib/site.server';
export const load = async ({ url }) => ({
	q: url.searchParams.get('q') ?? '',
	hits: await docs.search(url.searchParams.get('q') ?? '', { base: '/docs' })
});
```

```svelte
<script>
	import { SearchPage } from 'ogygia/content';
	let { data } = $props();
</script>

<SearchPage q={data.q} hits={data.hits} />
```

The same index, the same ranking, zero client JS: the fallback below the palette. **This site has one**: open `/docs/search` directly.

## Scoping and swapping

`docs.search(q, { in: [collection] })` narrows the query to one collection. That is applicable when a site carries several (docs + blog), and a box must search only one. The static index carries each collection. The worker filters by scope.

The engine itself is a **contract, not a dependency**. The default is Orama. `site({ search: { engine } })` swaps it for anything that can build and query the section documents: a hosted service, a different library, a test double.

::: details What a hit looks like
`SearchHit` is plain data, ready to render:

| field | meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `href` | deep link, baked for `base`; lands on the heading of the matched *section* |
| `title` | the page title |
| `section` | the nav group / top-level section label |
| `heading` | the heading of the matched section |
| `excerpt` | the matched prose, with `<mark>` around matched terms |
| `score` | the rank from the engine, with title matches boosted |
:::

## Do / don't

- **Do** ship the static index for a prerendered site. An on-device worker is faster to serve, and operates offline. The server never sees a keystroke.
- **Do** keep the fallback page. It is one server load. With it, search survives when JS is off, or not yet loaded.
- **Don't** mint a `search` remote *and* ship the index for the same box. Pick one path. The index is for the worker. The remote is for server queries.
- **Don't** build your own index off the raw bodies. `docs.emit.search()` splits by section. Thus a hit deep-links to the correct heading.
